<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Tysilio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Tysilio was a Welsh saint of the seventh century, said in tradition to be a son of Brochwel Ysgithrog, king of Powys. He is associated above all with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meifod">Meifod</a>, the mother church of Powys, and churches dedicated to him are found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, including Llandysilio on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>. His feast day is 8 November.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-tysilio.jpg" alt="Saint Tysilio" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Édouard Hue (User:EdouardHue) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buste_reliquaire_de_saint_Suliac,_Sizun,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
